<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alonso de Ojeda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1466–<a href="/years/1515">1515</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Alonso de Hojeda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alonso de Ojeda was a Spanish explorer who sailed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a>'s second voyage in 1493. On <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a> he captured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacique">cacique</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caonabo">Caonabo</a> by a ruse in 1495. He later led voyages along the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, naming Venezuela in 1499, and tried to found a colony at San Sebastián de Urabá in 1510, before dying poor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alonso-de-ojeda.jpg" alt="Alonso de Ojeda" width="320" height="462" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ignacio Castillo Cervantes · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Conquistador_Alonso_de_Ojeda.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alonso de Ojeda was born around 1466 in Torrejoncillo del Rey, New Castile. His father, Rodrigo de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huete">Huete</a>, was a minor noble who fought for Isabel in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-castilian-succession">War of the Castilian Succession</a>, and she took the son under her protection. In his youth he served the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medinaceli">Medinaceli</a>, Luis de la Cerda, and distinguished himself in the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>. Bartolomé de las Casas described him as small but with every bodily perfection.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Ojeda captained a ship on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a>'s second voyage, which left in September 1493. On <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a> he led armed parties, one of which brought back gold from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cibao">Cibao</a> in January 1494. At a river crossing he arrested a local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacique">cacique</a> on a charge of stolen clothes and cut the ears off one captive, an act that turned the local people against the Spaniards. According to Las Casas, he persuaded the cacique <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caonabo">Caonabo</a> to wear polished brass manacles as a mark of royalty and brought him to Columbus. After a rebellion late in 1494, a force of 500 led by Ojeda defeated the rebels, and some 1,500 were taken as slaves.</p><p>He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1496 and was commissioned by the Catholic Monarchs, without <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a>'s permission, to sail again. With Juan de la Cosa and Amerigo Vespucci he left on 18 May 1499 with three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravel">caravels</a> and explored the coast from the Gulf of Paria to the Guajira Peninsula. The name Venezuela, Little Venice, is said to come from stilt villages of the Wayuu people. A second voyage in 1502 founded Santa Cruz on the Guajira, the first Spanish settlement on the American mainland, but it lasted under three months, and Ojeda was imprisoned on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a> until 1504.</p><p>In 1508 he was made governor of New Andalusia. Landing near Cartagena in 1509, his party raided villages for slaves and suffered a heavy defeat at Turbaco, where Juan de la Cosa died. On 20 January 1510 he founded San Sebastián de Urabá, a fort that soon ran short of food. Ojeda left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> in charge and never returned; Pizarro abandoned the post.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ojeda was shipwrecked on the south coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and crossed it on foot, losing half his companions, before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panfilo-de-narvaez">Pánfilo de Narváez</a> rescued the survivors. He gave up his governorship and spent his last five years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a>, dying around 1515 in the monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a>, sick and poor. He asked to be buried beneath its door so that visitors would walk over his grave. Las Casas wrote that he had stolen much gold and pearls from the Indians. The city of Ciudad Ojeda, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-maracaibo">Lake Maracaibo</a>, is named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alonso de Ojeda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1495 <b>Alonso de Ojeda</b> captured him by a ruse, and he died at sea on the way to Spain.</blockquote><a href="/state/cacicazgo-of-maguana">Cacicazgo of Maguana</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Torrejoncillo del Rey, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/dominican-republic">the Dominican Republic</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.01,-2.57&amp;year=1497" aria-label="Torrejoncillo del Rey on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Alonso de Ojeda"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4832 4147h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4832 4147h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.01,-2.57&amp;year=1497">What was here in 1497? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
